From: Gabriel Tetar <gabriel@intercreate.io>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 11:27:06 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] usb: usb_c: fix the stack and add the Messages USB PD R3.2
 requires

Sixteen changes to the USB-C stack, carried as one patch: fixes to the
Policy Engine, the Protocol Layer and the timers, the DPM requests and
notifications a dual-role PPS port needs, and the Messages USB PD R3.2
requires. Parts 11, 12 and 16 also change the STM32 UCPD driver. Each
part was a separate patch; they apply in this order, and later parts
build on earlier ones. Where a later part changes what an earlier one
describes, the later part holds: part 11 resets the HardResetCounter
when Source_Capabilities are answered with GoodCRC, as the specification
does, where part 1 had it reset by an Explicit Contract, so a Sink that
never sends a Request gets Hard Resets for as long as it stays attached;
part 16 changes how Error Recovery is requested and how DR_Swap support
is known.

 1. fix AMS end, resets, Hard Reset count, roles and requests
 2. pe: let the DPM resend caps, re-request and give PPS status
 3. fix timer re-init and the discard of a waiting AMS start
 4. notify the DPM of sent capabilities, a silent partner and a Reject
 5. let a source send an Alert and answer Get_Status
 6. present the source Rp the contract calls for
 7. answer the information requests USB PD R3.2 requires
 8. meet USB PD R3.2 timing and message rules in the Policy Engine
 9. let a sink send an Alert and get its partner's Status
10. let a Dual-Role Power port swap its power role
11. fix the R3.2 deviations an audit of the stack found
12. support the BIST modes a USB PD port Shall support
13. let a port get its partner's Battery Status
14. answer Get_Source_Cap_Extended
15. answer Get_Manufacturer_Info
16. fix what a review of the carried stack found

1. fix AMS end, resets, Hard Reset count, roles and requests

Policy Engine and request bugs a port hits once it sources or holds a
contract: an AMS a Source starts never ends, Soft and Hard Resets
continue in sink states, a Sink that never requests gets Hard Resets
without end while one that stops answering after a Hard Reset gets no
more, an Accept that is not sent stalls the power transition, GoodCRC
carries the wrong Port Power Role on a dual-role port's first source
attach, a message sent again from PE_SRC_Ready or PE_SNK_Ready stalls,
misreads a discard or skips Collision Avoidance, Get_Source_Cap never
completes, and the single request node can be queued twice.

On entry to PE_SRC_Ready the Source Shall notify the Protocol Layer of
the end of the AMS, unless a Protocol Error that did not cause a Soft
Reset brought it there. The test was inverted: the AMS ended only after
such an error, and nothing sets that flag. Every source-initiated AMS
(Get_Sink_Cap, GotoMin) therefore stayed open until detach. In a PD 3.0
contract the Protocol Layer holds Rp at SinkTxNG for the length of an
AMS, so the Sink could not start one of its own (a PPS keep-alive
Request, Get_PPS_Status), and every later Source message waited tSinkTx
in PRL_Tx_Src_Pending first.

The Protocol Layer also lost the Collision Avoidance state:
PRL_Tx_Wait_for_Message_Request's entry cleared every transmit flag,
PRL_FLAGS_SINK_NG included, so its run state, which puts Rp back to
SinkTxOk once the AMS has ended, never found it set. The entry now
keeps PRL_FLAGS_SINK_NG and PRL_FLAGS_WAIT_SINK_OK.

PE_SRC_Ready also took DPM requests before PS_RDY was sent. While an
AMS that never ended held each message back for tSinkTx, the first
message of the AMS a request started replaced PS_RDY in the Protocol
Layer before it went out, and the Sink, waiting for PS_RDY in
PE_SNK_Transition_Sink, received an unexpected message. Otherwise
PS_RDY went out, but the new state took its completion for its own
message's: PE_SRC_Ready never recorded the contract (no Explicit
Contract, no PD_CONNECTED), and the new state went on before its own
message was sent. PE_SRC_Ready now takes no DPM request until PS_RDY is
sent; one that comes earlier is dropped, as any request is outside
PE_SRC_Ready. A PS_RDY that is discarded or not sent is a Protocol
Error during the power transition, which goes to PE_SRC_Hard_Reset.

The flag that PS_RDY is pending is cleared when PE_SRC_Ready is left and
by a Soft Reset, whose Protocol Layer reset drops a PS_RDY not yet sent,
so a stale flag cannot keep PE_SRC_Ready from taking DPM requests.
PE_SRC_Startup, entered after a Hard Reset as well as at attach, clears
the AMS, message received, transmission error, discard and Protocol
Error flags the previous negotiation left, and the Protocol Layer puts
Rp back to SinkTxOk before a reset or Hard Reset clears its transmit
flags, where an AMS cut short by either left it at SinkTxNG.

A received Soft Reset now ends the AMS too, so its Accept is not held
back as the first message of a new one. prl_reset() restores SinkTxOk
only while the Protocol Layer runs: once PD stops the TC owns the CC
lines, and prl_init() clears the flags before the next attach.

PE_SRC_Transition_Supply sent PS_RDY from its exit, so a Soft Reset
received during the transition still put PS_RDY on the wire after the
Sink's Soft_Reset. It now sends PS_RDY on the way to PE_SRC_Ready only,
and its exit just clears the request reply.

Its Protocol Error transition tested PE_FLAGS_PROTOCOL_ERROR, which
pe_report_error() sets only in the Soft Reset states, so an Accept that
was not sent, or that a Message from the Sink discarded, left the Policy
Engine in PE_SRC_Transition_Supply after it had already told the DPM to
transition the power supply. Either now goes to PE_SRC_Hard_Reset.

The Soft Reset and Hard Reset paths that the sink and source share
always continued in sink states, so a Source port left its source
states and its PD communication stopped:
- PE_Soft_Reset, once its Accept was sent, and PE_Send_Soft_Reset, once
  the Accept arrived, went to PE_SNK_Wait_for_Capabilities. A Source
  goes to PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities.
- A Protocol Error in either, a SenderResponseTimer timeout in
  PE_Send_Soft_Reset, and a Hard Reset request from the Device Policy
  Manager went to PE_SNK_Hard_Reset. A Source goes to
  PE_SRC_Hard_Reset.
- Hard Reset Signaling received went to PE_SNK_Transition_to_default.
  A Source goes to PE_SRC_Hard_Reset_Received, which already existed
  and was unreachable.

Each of these now goes to the source or the sink state by the port's
present power role, as pe_set_ready_state() already does for
PE_SRC_Ready and PE_SNK_Ready. PE_Send_Soft_Reset logs under that name
rather than PE_SNK_Send_Soft_Reset.

PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities never ran its SenderResponseTimer: its
submachine consumed the transmit-complete flag that the sender-response
parent state starts the timer on, so a sink that never answered left the
policy engine waiting in that state for good. Start the timer where the
submachine sees the transmission complete, as the specification asks.

With the timer running, a Sink that answers Source_Capabilities with
GoodCRC but never sends a Request gets a Hard Reset, and with it a cycle
of VBUS, every few seconds for as long as it stays attached:
PE_SRC_Hard_Reset never incremented the HardResetCounter, so no
nHardResetCount limit could apply.

PE_SRC_Hard_Reset now increments the HardResetCounter on entry. The
counter is reset by an Explicit Contract (PS_RDY sent) rather than by
the GoodCRC to Source_Capabilities: the spec resets it on every such
GoodCRC, which never lets the count grow for such a Sink. Linux's TCPM
makes the same choice. When the SenderResponseTimer times out in
PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities after more than nHardResetCount Hard Resets,
the port tells the DPM its partner is not responsive
(PORT_PARTNER_NOT_RESPONSIVE, as the sink side does) and goes to
PE_SRC_Disabled, keeping vSafe5V until detach; ErrorRecovery would
restart the attach and the Hard Resets with it. The state checks the
timer whatever else it received in that cycle.

The NoResponseTimer was started on a Hard Reset but never initialized
and never checked, so a Sink that stopped answering after a Hard Reset
got Source_Capabilities until the CapsCounter ran out, and no further
Hard Reset. It is now initialized once when the Policy Engine is set up,
stopped at each source attach, and checked in PE_SRC_Discovery and,
until GoodCRC, in PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities. When it times out the port
sends another Hard Reset or, past nHardResetCount, notifies
PORT_PARTNER_NOT_RESPONSIVE and goes to PE_SRC_Disabled. The spec asks
for ErrorRecovery instead once the Port Partners have been PD Connected,
which would loop for the same reason. The sender response super state
checked both limits on the SenderResponseTimer, which never runs in
PE_SRC_Discovery and which PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities now handles itself,
so those cases are removed. PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities returns
SMF_EVENT_HANDLED, so that super state's run cannot take a timeout that
falls between its own check and the super state's. Source_Capabilities
answered with GoodCRC now mark the Port Partners PD Connected
(PE_FLAGS_HAS_BEEN_PD_CONNECTED), which nothing reads yet.

pe_src_init() and pe_snk_init() set the data role through
pe_set_data_role(), which passes the Policy Engine's power role to
tcpc_set_roles() along with it. That power role was still the one of
the port's previous attach, or the reset value TC_ROLE_SINK on the
first: PE_SRC_Startup and PE_SNK_Startup set it only afterwards, and
neither tells the TCPC. Attached.SRC had set the TCPC's roles to Source
and DFP, so pe_src_init() put them back to Sink, and the TCPC built the
header of every message it generates itself, GoodCRC among them, with
the wrong Port Power Role until the next data role change. A
dual-role port's first source attach always did.

Both now set the power role before the data role.

PE_SRC_Transition_to_default and PE_SNK_Transition_to_default reset the
data role through pe_set_data_role() as well, so the TCPC also learns
the role a Hard Reset restores after a Data Role Swap.

PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities went to PE_SRC_Discovery whenever the
capabilities were not sent, since nothing sets PE_FLAGS_PD_CONNECTED.
That is right before a contract, but not for capabilities sent again
from PE_SRC_Ready in answer to Get_Source_Cap. With an Explicit
Contract, capabilities discarded by a Message from the Sink now notify
the DPM (MSG_DISCARDED) and return to PE_SRC_Ready, which handles that
Message, and capabilities that were not sent are a Protocol Error,
which sends a Soft Reset.

PE_SNK_Select_Capability entered from PE_SNK_Ready, as the Request sent
again after a Wait is, went back to PE_SNK_Ready on a discarded Request
but went on to read the Message that discarded it as the reply, and
sent a Soft Reset unless it was an Accept, Reject or Wait. It now
notifies the DPM and leaves the Message to PE_SNK_Ready. A Request that
was not sent left the policy engine in PE_SNK_Select_Capability for
good, since the SenderResponseTimer starts only once the Request is
sent; it is now a Protocol Error, which sends a Soft Reset.

Source_Capabilities under an Explicit Contract, or a Request, that is
not sent goes to a Hard Reset instead when a Soft Reset has completed
since the port was last in a Ready state: that Soft Reset did not
restore communication, and a partner that accepts Soft Resets but never
acknowledges the message would otherwise keep the port cycling through
them. The HardResetCounter bounds the Hard Resets.

sink_dpm_requests() and common_dpm_requests() marked a DPM-initiated AMS
for any DPM request, including one neither takes, such as a source
request that reaches a port now attached as a sink, so its next Message
waited for SinkTxOk. They now mark one only for a request they take.
PE_SNK_Ready sent a Request or a DR_Swap again after a Wait without
marking the AMS it starts, so a PD 3.0 Sink sent it without waiting
for SinkTxOk; both now mark it.
pe_send_ctrl_msg() and pe_send_data_msg() also clear a transmission
error or discard an earlier Message left behind, so the states above act
only on the outcome of their own Message.

PE_SNK_Get_Source_Cap matched the Source_Capabilities reply with
received_control_message(), which requires an empty message, so a
REQUEST_PE_GET_SRC_CAPS never reached PE_SNK_Evaluate_Capability and
the sink kept its contract. Match it as a data message.

usbc_request(), usbc_start() and usbc_suspend() queue the port's single
request node. Queueing it again before the port thread took it linked
the node to itself, and the port thread then took the same request on
every cycle; a request that came between the port thread taking the
node and reading its value replaced that value. The node is now queued
under a spinlock, and the port thread takes it and reads its value
under the same lock. While it is queued, usbc_request() returns -EBUSY,
except for a Type-C error recovery request, which the stack itself
raises and does not retry: it replaces a queued public request, and is
refused only behind a queued start or suspend request. A start or
suspend request replaces whatever is queued, so neither call fails.

2. pe: let the DPM resend caps, re-request and give PPS status

Two Device Policy Manager requests, REQUEST_PE_SEND_SRC_CAPS and
REQUEST_PE_SNK_REQUEST, and the PPS_Status reply a PPS source owes a
Sink.

A Device Policy Manager that changes its Source Capabilities while a
sink holds an explicit contract has no way to offer them: the source
DPM requests cover only Get_Sink_Cap and GotoMin, and a Soft Reset,
meant for protocol errors, is no substitute. A sink holding a PPS
contract must send a new Request at least every tPPSRequest, and the
only sink DPM request is Get_Source_Cap.

Add REQUEST_PE_SEND_SRC_CAPS, which takes PE_SRC_Ready to
PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities, and REQUEST_PE_SNK_REQUEST, which takes
PE_SNK_Ready to PE_SNK_Select_Capability with a fresh Request Data
Object from the DPM. Both mark a DPM-initiated AMS. They take the paths
PE_SRC_Ready and PE_SNK_Ready already take for Get_Source_Cap and for a
Request sent again after a Wait, including what those do when the
message is discarded or not sent.

A source that offers a Programmable Power Supply Shall answer
Get_PPS_Status with a PPS_Status message, but PE_SRC_Ready sent
Not_Supported for it, and the Protocol Layer could not transmit an
extended message at all: every header it built had the extended bit
clear.

Add union pd_ppssdb, the PPS Status Data Block, and a policy callback
that fills it (usbc_set_policy_cb_get_pps_status()). With the callback
set, PE_SRC_Ready takes a Get_PPS_Status to the new
PE_SRC_Give_PPS_Status state, which sends PPS_Status as a single chunk:
the Extended Message Header (chunked, four bytes of data) followed by
the PPSSDB, padded to two data objects, and returns to PE_SRC_Ready
once it is sent. Without the callback the reply is still
Not_Supported.

The Protocol Layer gains prl_send_ext_msg(), which marks the message as
extended in the header it builds; the caller places the extended header
at the start of the data, so only messages that fit in one chunk are
supported.

PE_SRC_Give_PPS_Status returns to PE_SRC_Ready once PPS_Status is sent
or discarded by a Message from the Sink, which PE_SRC_Ready then
handles; a PPS_Status that was not sent is a Protocol Error, which sends
a Soft Reset. pe_send_ext_msg() clears a transmission error or discard
an earlier Message left behind, as the other send functions do.

3. fix timer re-init and the discard of a waiting AMS start

Two bugs below the Policy Engine: re-initializing a running USB-C timer
corrupts the kernel's timeout list, and a Message received while the
first Message of our AMS waits in the Protocol Layer did not discard it.

The Type-C, Policy Engine and Protocol Layer state machines call
usbc_timer_init() for their timers on every attach, and the Protocol
Layer on every reset, which a Soft Reset or PE_SRC_Startup requests. A
timer can still be running then: PRL_Tx_Wait_for_PHY_response starts the
100 ms tTxTimeout, and a Soft Reset sent in the same cycle as a Request
has the Protocol Layer initialized again microseconds later.
k_timer_init() resets the timer's timeout node while it is still linked
in the kernel's timeout list; the state's exit then calls
k_timer_stop(), which finds the node unlinked and leaves the list
pointing at it, and the next k_timer_start() links it a second time. The
timeout list is corrupt from then on, and the kernel faults or hangs in
sys_dlist_remove().

usbc_timer_init() now initializes the kernel timer only the first time.
Later calls stop it and reset its timeout and its started and expired
flags, which also keeps k_timer_init() from adding the timer to the
object core list again when CONFIG_OBJ_CORE_TIMER is set.

A Message received while one of ours is waiting to be transmitted
Shall discard ours (PRL_Tx_Discard_Message). The Protocol Layer only
flagged it, still built and transmitted the Message, and reported the
discard once the PHY had answered, at least one cycle after the Policy
Engine had taken the received Message. A state waiting for its own
Message's outcome therefore read the partner's Message as the reply,
and the partner received a Message it was not expecting. In
PRL_Tx_Snk_Pending, which clears PRL_FLAGS_MSG_XMIT when it starts to
wait for SinkTxOk, the received Message was not even flagged, and the
Request still went out once Rp returned to SinkTxOk.

When the first Message of an AMS this port started has not been passed
to the PHY yet (still in PRL_Tx_Wait_for_Message_Request, Src_Source_Tx,
Src_Pending, Snk_Start_of_AMS or Snk_Pending), a received SOP Message
other than Ping now drops it, returns the transmitter to
PRL_Tx_Wait_for_Message_Request, which also ends Collision Avoidance,
and reports the discard before the received Message reaches the Policy
Engine. A Soft_Reset is never dropped. Other pending Messages keep the
previous handling.

The states that start an AMS now all act on the discard before reading a
received Message. PE_Get_Sink_Cap, PE_SNK_Get_Source_Cap and the GotoMin
path of PE_SRC_Transition_Supply leave for the Ready state instead of
waiting for a transmission that will not happen, and tell the DPM
(MSG_DISCARDED); PE_DRS_Send_Swap checks the discard first, where it
read the partner's Message as the reply and requested ErrorRecovery.
Each of them, and PE_SNK_Get_Source_Cap, sends a Soft Reset when its
Message is not sent, where it waited for good. PE_Get_Sink_Cap starts
its SenderResponseTimer where its submachine takes the transmission's
completion, which the timer's parent state never saw, and its timeout
returns to the Ready state with SENDER_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT to the DPM
rather than sending the Soft Reset of the branch that this made
reachable.

4. notify the DPM of sent capabilities, a silent partner and a Reject

Three Policy Engine events the Device Policy Manager could not see:

- Source_Capabilities answered with GoodCRC. PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities
  now notifies SOURCE_CAPABILITIES_SENT, a new enum usbc_policy_notify_t
  value, when the Protocol Layer reports the message sent. From then on
  the Sink holds those capabilities and owes a Request; before it, a
  discard or a transmission error can still drop them. A DPM that
  changes its capabilities at run time needs to know which set the Sink
  has: the one it handed to the PE, or the one before.
- A partner that never answers Source_Capabilities. PE_SRC_Discovery
  went to PE_SRC_Disabled after nCapsCount sends without GoodCRC (a
  Type-C sink that is not PD capable) with no notification. It now
  notifies PORT_PARTNER_NOT_RESPONSIVE first, as the NoResponseTimer
  and SenderResponseTimer paths to PE_SRC_Disabled already do.
- A Reject to a Request. PE_SNK_Select_Capability went back to
  PE_SNK_Ready or PE_SNK_Wait_for_Capabilities without telling the DPM,
  which had already prepared for the requested contract (a Sink lowers
  its input current before asking for less). It now notifies
  MSG_REJECTED_RECEIVED, as PE_DRS_Send_Swap does for a rejected
  DR_Swap. A Wait still calls the wait_notify callback (WAIT_SINK_REQUEST)
  when there is an Explicit Contract.

A DPM whose notify callback asserts on values it does not know must
handle SOURCE_CAPABILITIES_SENT once it uses a source role.

5. let a source send an Alert and answer Get_Status

USB PD R3.2 makes the Alert AMS Required of an initiator that supports
SPR PPS Mode, and the Get Status AMS Required of a responding Source
port that supports Alert. A Source that answers Get_PPS_Status Shall
support sending the Alert Message. Every Source port Should also send an
Alert, with the Source Reducing Capabilities extended event, within
tReducePowerAlert (2000-2400 ms) before it sends reduced Source
Capabilities. The Policy Engine supported neither: an Alert could not be
sent, and Get_Status was answered with Not_Supported.

- REQUEST_PE_SEND_ALERT takes PE_SRC_Ready to PE_SRC_Send_Source_Alert,
  which sends an Alert with the Alert Data Object a new DPM callback
  returns (usbc_set_policy_cb_get_alert()). The PE calls it as it hands
  the Alert to the Protocol Layer, so the DPM knows when the Alert went
  out. Without it, or without the get_status callback below (a Source
  port that supports Alert Shall answer Get_Status), the request is
  ignored. The ADO bits are in usbc_pd.h (PD_ADO_*). The request is
  ignored below PD Revision 3.0: PD 2.0 has no Alert, a 2.0 sink would
  answer it with Reject, and PE_SRC_Ready takes an unexpected Reject as
  a reason for a Soft Reset. An Alert Data Object of 0 (the DPM's events
  went away while the request waited) sends nothing and returns to
  PE_SRC_Ready: an Alert Shall only be sent for a status change.
- Once the Alert is sent the PE waits in PE_SRC_Wait_for_Get_Status for
  tSenderResponse (27 ms, its own timer: the shared SenderResponseTimer
  runs 5.5 s, and the DPM's requests wait for PE_SRC_Ready). A Get_Status
  goes to PE_Give_Status; any other Message goes back to PE_SRC_Ready,
  which handles it.
- PE_SRC_Ready answers Get_Status from PE_Give_Status, which sends a
  single-chunk Status with the SOP Status Data Block another new DPM
  callback fills (usbc_set_policy_cb_get_status(); struct pd_sdb and
  PD_SDB_* in usbc_pd.h). Without the callback, or below PD Revision
  3.0, where Get_Status is Reserved, the reply stays Not_Supported,
  which is Reject at Revision 2.0. The OCP, OTP and OVP Event Flags
  Shall be cleared after the Status is sent, so a Status answered with
  GoodCRC notifies STATUS_SENT, a new notification, on which the DPM
  clears the flags it reported; a discarded or unsent Status keeps them
  for the next one.
- An Alert or Status that a Message from the Sink discards goes back to
  PE_SRC_Ready, which handles that Message; one that is not sent sends a
  Soft Reset. An Alert that leaves PE_SRC_Send_Source_Alert unsent, by a
  discard, a transmission error, a reset or a detach, notifies
  ALERT_DISCARDED, a new notification, from the state's exit: the
  Alert's events stay the DPM's until it is sent, and the DPM, which
  handed them over as the PE took the Alert, may ask again.
  MSG_DISCARDED would not tell it which of its messages was dropped, and
  an Alert can be discarded for tSinkTx after the PE takes it.

6. present the source Rp the contract calls for

During an Explicit Contract at PD 3.0 a Source uses Rp for Collision
Avoidance: SinkTxNG (1.5 A) while it holds the bus, SinkTxOk (3.0 A) to
release it (USB PD R3.2). During the Default or Implicit Contract Rp
specifies the Type-C Current and Shall not be used for Collision
Avoidance. Zephyr only selected the Device Policy Manager's Rp on
entering the Type-C CC super state, and the Protocol Layer put SinkTxOk
on CC only at the end of an AMS the source itself had started (when it
had set SinkTxNG). Two gaps followed:

- After a Hard Reset under a PD 3.0 contract the port kept presenting
  SinkTxOk (3.0 A) whatever the DPM chose, and a DPM that sizes its
  first Source_Capabilities by its own Rp choice offered less than CC
  advertised. PE_SRC_Transition_to_default now asks the DPM for its Rp
  (policy_cb_get_src_rp, TC_RP_USB without it) and selects it, after the
  power supply is told to reset and before the port comes back at
  vSafe5V.
- A contract made while the DPM's Type-C Rp was below 3.0 A (the first
  one, or the one after a Hard Reset) kept that Rp until the source
  started an AMS of its own, so a PD 3.0 sink read SinkTxNG or Default
  and Shall Not start an AMS: no PPS keep-alive Request, no Get_Status.
  The Protocol Layer now presents SinkTxOk whenever no AMS is running
  under a PD 3.0 Explicit Contract, once per contract (a new flag,
  cleared with the others at reset), as PRL_Tx_Src_Sink_Tx does at the
  end of every AMS.

7. answer the information requests USB PD R3.2 requires

USB PD R3.2 makes these Responders Required, and the Policy Engine
answered each with Not_Supported:
- Get Revision, of every port: a Revision Message.
- Get Source Information, of a port that can operate as Source: a
  Source_Info Message with SIDO1 and SIDO2, also while a Dual-Role port
  sinks.
- Get Sink Capabilities Extended, of a port that can operate as Sink: a
  Sink_Capabilities_Extended Message, also while a Dual-Role port
  sources.
- Get Battery Capabilities and Get Battery Status, of a product that
  contains batteries: Battery_Capabilities and Battery_Status, in either
  power role.
- Get Sink Capabilities and Get Source Capabilities of a Dual-Role port
  in its other power role: Sink_Capabilities from PE_SRC_Ready and
  Source_Capabilities from PE_SNK_Ready.

PE_SRC_Ready and PE_SNK_Ready now hand these requests to PE_GIVE_INFO,
one state for the nine Give states the specification draws: on entry it
builds the reply and sends it, and it returns to the Ready state once
the reply is sent. A reply that a Message from the partner discards, or
one that is not sent, is a Protocol Error and sends a Soft Reset: the
partner's AMS was waiting for it, so the Message that came instead is an
Unexpected Message during an AMS.

The Revision is the stack's own, R3.2 V1.2 (PD_RMDO, 0x32120000). The
rest comes from new DPM callbacks: usbc_set_policy_cb_get_src_info(),
_get_snk_cap_ext(), _get_battery_cap() and _get_battery_status(), with
union pd_sido1/pd_sido2, struct pd_skedb, struct pd_bcdb and union
pd_bsdo in usbc_pd.h. A request without its callback keeps the
Not_Supported reply. The Dual-Role replies use the get_src_caps and
get_snk_cap callbacks the port already has. The Battery reference of
Get_Battery_Cap and Get_Battery_Status reaches the callback as sent; a
request without one passes PD_BATTERY_REF_MAX, which no battery has, so
the DPM answers with an invalid reference. Battery requests are extended
Messages: only a single chunk is answered. Below PD Revision 3.0, where
these Message types are Reserved and the Extended bit too, only the
Dual-Role Get_Source_Cap and Get_Sink_Cap are answered; the rest gets
the Reject a PD 2.0 port sends for an unsupported Message. The stack has
no Chunking layer, which a port that handles no multi-chunk Message May
omit; a longer one still waits for the ChunkingNotSupportedTimer and
gets Not_Supported.

PE_SNK_Give_Sink_Cap ignored a transmission error; it now sends a Soft
Reset, as it already did when a Message from the Source discarded the
Sink_Capabilities, and as the other replies do.

The enums gain Get_Source_Info, Get_Revision, Source_Info, Revision and
Sink_Capabilities_Extended.

8. meet USB PD R3.2 timing and message rules in the Policy Engine

Four places where the Policy Engine did not follow USB PD R3.2:

- The SenderResponseTimer ran 5.5 s (tNoResponse) instead of
  tSenderResponse, 27-50 ms. A sink that never answered
  Source_Capabilities, or a source that never answered a Request, a
  Get_Source_Cap, a Get_Sink_Cap, a DR_Swap or a Soft_Reset, held the
  port in that state for 5.5 s before the Hard Reset or the return to
  Ready the specification asks for after tSenderResponse. The timer now
  runs tSenderResponse's maximum less two state machine cycles (40 ms at
  the default 5 ms): the Policy Engine starts it one cycle after the
  GoodCRC at the latest and sees it expire one cycle late at the latest,
  so a response is given 40-50 ms. A BUILD_ASSERT keeps it at or above
  tSenderResponse's minimum. The PD_T_SENDER_RESPONSE_* limits are
  R3.2's, 27 and 50 ms (they were R3.0's 24 and 30).
  PE_SRC_Wait_for_Get_Status, which ran a 27 ms timer of its own while
  the shared one ran 5.5 s, now runs the SenderResponseTimer too.
- A Message received in PE_SRC_Transition_Supply was left for
  PE_SRC_Ready to handle once PS_RDY was sent. A Message received while
  the power supply transitions Shall be answered with Hard Reset
  Signaling; it now is, as a discarded or unsent Accept already was.
- GotoMin is Deprecated and no longer Valid, and REQUEST_PE_GOTO_MIN
  made the source send it. The request is now ignored,
  PE_SRC_Transition_Supply only follows an Accept, and its GotoMin paths
  are gone.
- PE_SNK_Ready ignored a Not_Supported Message. On entry to
  PE_SNK_Not_Supported_Received the Policy Engine Shall inform the
  Device Policy Manager; it now notifies MSG_NOT_SUPPORTED_RECEIVED, as
  PE_SRC_Ready does.

9. let a sink send an Alert and get its partner's Status

USB PD R3.2 makes the Get PPS Status AMS Required of an Initiator that
supports SPR PPS Mode, which is the PPS Sink, and a Sink that supports
it Shall support receiving the Alert Message. The Alert AMS is Required
of an Initiator that supports SPR PPS Mode too. The Policy Engine had no
Get_PPS_Status or Get_Status Initiator, answered a received Alert with
Not_Supported, and could send an Alert only as a source.

- PE_Get_Status and PE_SNK_Get_PPS_Status, on new DPM requests
  REQUEST_PE_GET_STATUS (either role) and REQUEST_PE_GET_PPS_STATUS
  (sink): send the request, run the SenderResponseTimer, and report the
  Status or PPS Status through new callbacks
  (usbc_set_policy_cb_set_status(), _set_pps_status()), NULL when the
  partner sends none (Not_Supported, Reject, a timeout, or a request
  that a Message from the partner discarded). Any other reply is a
  Protocol Error and sends a Soft Reset, as an unsent request does. The
  DPM Shall learn the outcome on exit: a reset or detach that ends the
  state reports NULL too.
- PE_SNK_Source_Alert_Received and PE_SRC_Sink_Alert_Received, embedded
  in the Ready states: an Alert at PD 3.0 or later goes to a new
  callback (usbc_set_policy_cb_alert_received()), and when it returns
  true the port gets the partner's Status. That Get_Status belongs to
  the partner's Alert AMS, so it is sent without starting an AMS of
  ours: a sink does not wait for SinkTxOk, which the source holds back
  while it waits for it. Since it is not the first Message of an AMS, a
  Message that discards it is a Protocol Error and sends a Soft Reset; a
  Get_Status the DPM asked for goes back to the Ready state instead,
  which handles the Message. Without the callback an Alert is still
  answered with Not_Supported.
- PE_SNK_Send_Sink_Alert and PE_SNK_Wait_for_Get_Status:
  REQUEST_PE_SEND_ALERT now works as a sink too, with the same get_alert
  callback; a Get_Status within tSenderResponse is answered with the
  sink's Status, from the get_status callback through PE_GIVE_INFO,
  which notifies STATUS_SENT once it is sent; so is a
  Get_Battery_Status, the Alert AMS's other optional sequence. A
  Not_Supported, from a Source without the Alert Responder (Recommended
  only), ends the AMS. A Source whose DPM does not ask for the status
  goes back to PE_SRC_Ready and may start a new AMS at once, so any
  other Message is handled in PE_SNK_Ready. A Status that a Message
  discards is a Protocol Error and sends a Soft Reset. An Alert that
  leaves PE_SNK_Send_Sink_Alert unsent notifies ALERT_DISCARDED from the
  state's exit, as the source's does; one not acknowledged sends a Soft
  Reset. get_alert and get_status are no longer source-only, and a sink
  sends no Alert without get_status either.
- A sink ignores these requests below PD Revision 3.0, which has none of
  these Messages, and answers a Get_Status there with Reject, as for any
  Reserved Message.
- usbc_pd.h gains the Alert Data Object's Fixed and Hot Swappable
  Batteries fields and the Status Data Block's Present Input and Present
  Battery Input bits, which a sink fills too.

10. let a Dual-Role Power port swap its power role

USB PD R3.2 makes the Power Role Swap AMS Required of a DRP, as
Initiator and Responder. The Policy Engine answered PR_Swap with
Not_Supported and could not send one.

The Policy Engine, with CONFIG_USBC_CSM_DRP, now follows the Power Role
Swap rules of USB PD R3.2:
- From PE_SRC_Ready or PE_SNK_Ready a received PR_Swap goes to
  PE_PRS_EVALUATE_SWAP, which asks the DPM (CHECK_POWER_ROLE_SWAP) and
  sends Accept or Reject (a reply that a Message discards, or that is
  not sent, is a Protocol Error and sends a Soft Reset); a new request,
  REQUEST_PE_PR_SWAP, goes to PE_PRS_SEND_SWAP, which sends PR_Swap and
  waits tSenderResponse for Accept, Reject or Wait. After a Reject, or a
  Not_Supported from a partner that cannot swap, the request is ignored
  until the next Explicit Contract negotiation or a Data Role Swap, and
  for tPRSwapWait after a Wait; the DPM is told of each, and of no reply
  (SENDER_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT).
- Source to Sink: the port waits tSrcTransition, asks the DPM to turn
  the Source off (the src_en callback), waits for vSafe0V within
  tSrcSwapStdby, counted from the end of tSrcTransition however long the
  DPM takes, asserts Rd, sends PS_RDY with Port Power Role Sink, and
  waits the PSSourceOnTimer, started at that PS_RDY's GoodCRC, for the
  New Source's PS_RDY, then PE_SNK_Startup.
- Sink to Source: the port runs the PSSourceOffTimer and tells the DPM
  to turn the sink off (POWER_CHANGE_0A0); on the Initial Source's
  PS_RDY it asserts Rp, asks the DPM to turn the Source on, sends PS_RDY
  with Port Power Role Source once VBUS is at vSafe5V within
  tSrcTransOn, which counts from the request and also bounds a DPM that
  takes long, then PE_SRC_Startup, which waits tSwapSourceStart before
  Source_Capabilities.
- A timeout, or a PS_RDY that is not sent, ends the swap in USB Type-C
  Error Recovery; the DPM gets PROTOCOL_ERROR first, so it knows the
  swap failed before the port detaches. The swap stays on until the
  Type-C layer takes the ErrorRecovery request. A Message received
  during the power transition is a Protocol Error that is never answered
  with a Soft Reset, and the specification handles it by role: a port in
  the Source role triggers Error Recovery directly, one in the Sink role
  a Hard Reset. The two sections disagree on the same event, so each
  swap state follows the rule for the role the port holds in it: Error
  Recovery in PE_PRS_SRC_SNK_Transition_to_off and
  PE_PRS_SNK_SRC_Source_on, Hard Reset in PE_PRS_SRC_SNK_Wait_Source_on
  and PE_PRS_SNK_SRC_Transition_to_off.
- The Explicit Contract ends at the role flip, so the New Source's Rp is
  the Type-C Current until the first new contract, not SinkTxOk. A port
  that started the swap as the Source (with SinkTxNG on CC for its
  PR_Swap) does not put Rp back when its Protocol Layer is reset as the
  New Sink: the Protocol Layer re-asserts SinkTxOk only as the Source.
  PE_SNK_Startup ends the AMS, so the New Sink's first Request does not
  wait for SinkTxOk.
- The four power-transition states share a parent whose exit stops the
  swap timer and ends the Type-C swap, so a Hard Reset, a received
  Soft_Reset or a detach in mid-swap cannot leave the Type-C layer
  ignoring VBUS loss or open CC. An Error Recovery, a Disabled or a
  suspend request that reaches the Type-C layer from elsewhere (the DPM
  after a supply fault, or the Protocol Layer) ends the swap there too,
  before the attached state's exit, which then turns PD and the supply
  off as for any detach. PE_SNK_Startup and PE_SRC_Startup reset the
  Protocol Layer, and PE_SRC_Startup the CapsCounter, as required after
  a swap.
- The data role and VCONN stay as they are.

The Type-C layer keeps the swap out of its attach and detach handling:
tc_pr_swap_start() marks a swap, tc_pr_swap_set_role() asserts Rd or Rp
and moves between Attached.SRC and Attached.SNK, and tc_pr_swap_end()
ends it (ErrorRecovery does too). During a swap the Attached states keep
PD running, leave the source and sink to the Policy Engine, set only the
power role on the TCPC, and do not take VBUS loss or open CC for a
detach.

A PPC, which the board this was written for does not have, is not
switched by the swap, and VCONN, which that board does not supply,
stays with the port that had it.

The timers use a range's minimum, and a maximum less two state machine
cycles, since the Policy Engine starts and checks them one cycle late at
the most. The PR_Swap timing parameters are new in usbc_pd.h.

11. fix the R3.2 deviations an audit of the stack found

An audit of the Policy Engine, Protocol Layer and Type-C layer against
USB PD R3.2 found these deviations from a Shall.

Policy Engine, source:
- The HardResetCounter is reset when Source_Capabilities are answered
  with GoodCRC, and only there and at power up; it was reset at PS_RDY
  instead. A SenderResponseTimer timeout in PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities
  always sends a Hard Reset; past nHardResetCount it went to
  PE_SRC_Disabled. When the NoResponseTimer times out past
  nHardResetCount the port goes to ErrorRecovery if the Port Partners
  have been PD Connected during this attach, else to PE_SRC_Disabled; it
  always went to Disabled.
- A Message other than a Request after Source_Capabilities is a Protocol
  Error and sends a Soft Reset; it was ignored.
- PE_SRC_Wait_New_Capabilities is a state of its own that goes to
  PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities once the DPM asks to send them; the port went
  to PE_SUSPEND and ignored the request.
- PE_SRC_Transition_Supply notifies TRANSITION_PS at the Accept's
  GoodCRC: tSrcTransition, after which the Source moves VBUS, counts
  from it. A sent Message now runs the Policy Engine again without the
  state machine's sleep, so the notification follows the GoodCRC within
  the cycle instead of 5 ms later. It notified on queuing the Accept, so
  the power supply could start moving after an Accept the Sink never
  acknowledged, inside the Hard Reset that follows. A Protocol Error
  once TRANSITION_PS is out notifies PROTOCOL_ERROR before the Hard
  Reset, so the DPM can drop a transition that has not started (the Sink
  keeps the current Contract for tSnkHardResetPrepare).
- When a Message from the Sink discards the first Message of the
  Source's AMS, PE_SRC_Ready's entry does not notify the Protocol Layer
  of the end of the AMS: SinkTxNG holds until the Sink's Message is
  handled, either at the next PE_SRC_Ready entry or once PE_SRC_Ready
  has handled it without leaving. The flag the entry tested was never
  set, so Rp went back to SinkTxOk at once.
- A Reject or Wait in PE_SRC_Capability_Response that is not sent, or
  that a Message from the Sink discards, sends a Soft Reset; the state
  waited forever.
- PE_SRC_Ready entry notifies SRC_READY_ENTERED, a new notification,
  when a Message received from the Sink caused the entry: under a PPS
  contract the SourcePPSCommTimer Shall then be initialized and run, and
  the DPM, which knows the contract, runs it. Its exit notifies
  SRC_READY_EXITED, another new notification, when a received Message
  takes the Source out of PE_SRC_Ready: the timer Shall stop then, so a
  BIST Test Data mode, which sends no further Message, does not end in
  its Hard Reset. An AMS the Source starts itself, such as an Alert,
  neither stops nor restarts the timer unless the Sink sends a Message
  in it, so a silent Sink still meets tPPSTimeout.
- The Port Partners stay PD Connected across a Power Role Swap or a Soft
  Reset, and a sink that receives Source_Capabilities is PD Connected
  too: a new flag, cleared by a Hard Reset, tracks it.
  Source_Capabilities with no GoodCRC go to PE_SRC_Discovery only when
  the partners are not PD Connected; after a Power Role Swap they send a
  Soft Reset, and a Message that discards them without an Explicit
  Contract does too; after a Soft Reset they send Hard Reset Signaling
  (below). A port that has sunk is PD Connected for the
  NoResponseTimer's ErrorRecovery after a swap.
- A Request for a PDO the Sink has not seen is rejected before the DPM
  sees it: the count of PDOs offered is taken when Source_Capabilities
  are answered with GoodCRC, so after a set that a Message from the Sink
  discards, the Sink's Request is checked against the set it holds.
- PE_SRC_Disabled ignores a Soft_Reset: the Source is unresponsive to
  USB Power Delivery messaging there, but not to Hard Reset Signaling.
  It went back to PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities.
- A Hard Reset sent while one is already under way restarts the
  PSHardResetTimer and the NoResponseTimer: the Source moves VBUS
  tPSHardReset after the last Hard Reset Signaling. Only the shared
  parent state's entry started them, which a second Hard Reset does not
  run. (Reception is off during a Hard Reset, so none is received then.)
- A Source with a Dual-Role Data port starts DR_Swap from PE_SRC_Ready
  when the DPM asks (REQUEST_PE_DR_SWAP): the initiator is Required for
  a DRD; only PE_SNK_Ready took the request. A Wait answer is told to
  the DPM, which asks again: only a Sink resends by itself.
- A Soft_Reset received in PE_SRC_Ready notifies SRC_READY_EXITED, as
  any received Message that takes the Source out of PE_SRC_Ready does.
- A Get_Battery_Status in PE_SRC_Wait_for_Get_Status that the port does
  not answer gets Not_Supported, not a Soft Reset.
- PE_SRC_Wait_New_Capabilities drops a Message received while it waits,
  so the next Source_Capabilities do not take it for their reply.

Policy Engine, sink:
- A Hard Reset's VBUS drop is not a Detach: Attached.SNK ignores VBUS
  loss from PE_SNK_Transition_to_default until VBUS is back, and
  PE_SNK_Discovery waits for vSafe0V (within tPSHardReset and tSafe0V)
  before it takes VBUS as present again, then for vSafe5V within
  tSrcRecover and tSrcTurnOn. Before, every Hard Reset re-attached the
  port, which reset the HardResetCounter, and Discovery took the old
  VBUS for the new one. A sink whose Policy Engine gave up on an
  unresponsive partner does not complete a later Hard Reset, so for it
  the VBUS loss is still a detach, which restarts the Protocol Layer and
  the Policy Engine on the next attach. A Hard Reset a Sink sends for a
  Protocol Error during a Power Role Swap's power transition, with VBUS
  near vSafe0V, is not a Detach either: PE_SNK_Hard_Reset marks the VBUS
  drop as the Hard Reset's on entry, so the swap ending no longer lets
  Attached.SNK detach before the Hard Reset completes, whoever asked for
  it. PE_SNK_Ready ends that marking: under a contract VBUS is the
  Source's again, also when a Soft Reset, not Discovery, led there.
- PE_SNK_Transition_Sink notifies MSG_ACCEPT_RECEIVED on entry: the
  Policy Engine Shall then request the DPM to transition the Sink's
  power supply to the new power level. The DPM heard of the new contract
  only at PS_RDY.
- After a Soft Reset, a Message other than Source_Capabilities in
  PE_SNK_Wait_for_Capabilities is a Protocol Error the Soft Reset did
  not correct and gets Hard Reset Signaling; it was dropped.
- A Message other than Source_Capabilities in PE_SNK_Get_Source_Cap is a
  Protocol Error and sends a Soft Reset; it was ignored. A timeout there
  notifies SENDER_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT: the DPM Shall learn the outcome.

Policy Engine, common:
- A Message other than Accept after our Soft_Reset sends a Hard Reset; a
  discarded Accept of a received Soft_Reset does too. PE_Send_Soft_Reset
  forgets a Message, discard or error from before its Soft_Reset, also
  one the reset Protocol Layer reads before the Soft_Reset goes out, so
  the Message that discarded ours and led to the Soft Reset is not taken
  for its reply: a Hard Reset follows only a Soft Reset that fails. It
  also stops the SenderResponseTimer of the AMS that failed, which
  shares its parent state and could expire before the Soft_Reset's own
  started. A Soft_Reset that a received Message discards is sent again:
  the state ends only on an Accept, a timeout or a transmission error,
  and going back to Ready left the partner unreset with its MessageIDs
  out of step with ours.
- A Message other than Accept, Reject, Wait or Not_Supported after
  DR_Swap sends a Soft Reset: ErrorRecovery is only for a Protocol Error
  while the data roles change. Not_Supported, the answer of a partner
  that is not a DRD (Unsupported Sequence), is told to the DPM like a
  Reject.
- DR_Swap replies and Not_Supported that are not sent send a Soft Reset;
  they waited forever.
- A source answers Get_PPS_Status below PD Revision 3.0, where it is
  Reserved, with Reject, as for any Reserved Message; it sent a PPS
  Status, an Extended Message that PD 2.0 does not have.
- A reply that a Message from the partner discards, in
  PE_SRC_Give_Status, PE_SRC_Give_PPS_Status, PE_Send_Not_Supported, and
  PE_SRC_Send_Capabilities when it answers a Get_Source_Cap, is an
  Unexpected Message during an AMS and sends a Soft Reset: the partner's
  AMS was waiting for the reply. They went back to the Ready state,
  which left the partner without its reply and unreset. Only a Message
  that arrives before the first Message of an AMS goes out is handled in
  the Ready state, so Source_Capabilities that the Source sends of its
  own accord still go back to PE_SRC_Ready.
- PE_SRC_Wait_for_Get_Status takes what the Alert AMS allows: a
  Get_Status, or a Get_Battery_Status answered through PE_GIVE_INFO; a
  Not_Supported, from a Sink without the Alert Responder (Recommended
  only), ends the AMS. The Sink cannot start an AMS of its own while Rp
  is SinkTxNG, so any other Message is a Protocol Error and sends a Soft
  Reset; it went back to PE_SRC_Ready.
- PE_SRC_Discovery stops a SenderResponseTimer left running by a sibling
  under the shared parent state, which would otherwise expire there.
- Source_Capabilities and Sink_Capabilities sent at PD Revision 2.0 end
  before the first APDO: all data in a Message Shall be consistent with
  its Specification Revision. A partner's Revision 1.0 is read as 2.0. A
  Request whose Object Position names a PDO that was not sent, such as
  an APDO left out at 2.0, is rejected before the DPM sees it, so its
  RDO is never decoded against an APDO the Sink never saw. A Request
  whose Object Position is above 7, which only an EPR_Request may use,
  gets Hard Reset Signaling; it was rejected. union pd_rdo now reads the
  Object Position as the four bits the specification gives it, 31..28:
  it read three and a Reserved bit, so a Request at 9..11 passed for one
  at 1..3.
- PE_Send_Soft_Reset ends the AMS that failed, as PE_Soft_Reset does:
  the Sink's Request after the Soft Reset waited for SinkTxOk as if it
  started a new AMS, so a Source holding SinkTxNG through the Soft Reset
  AMS saw no Request and sent a Hard Reset.
- A Protocol Error between an accepted Soft Reset and the next Ready
  state sends Hard Reset Signaling: the Soft Reset did not correct the
  error. A Protocol Error reported before the Soft Reset's Accept was
  handled ends with it. Any other Message after the Source_Capabilities
  or the Request that follow it, or a Message that discards them, sent
  another Soft_Reset, so a partner that kept answering wrongly looped
  through Soft Resets for ever.
- A sink sends no Request within tSinkRequest of a Wait, whoever asks
  for it: the timer runs after every Wait, and a DPM request inside it
  waits for its end. The timer stops when any other Message arrives and
  during a Hard Reset; a Request it held still waits out tSinkRequest
  from the Wait, and new Source_Capabilities or a Hard Reset end that
  hold.
- Ping, Deprecated, is ignored in every state: it got a Soft Reset or a
  Hard Reset during an AMS, and stopped the SinkRequestTimer. Only a DPM
  that asked to be retried started the timer, and any other Request went
  out at once.
- A reported error or discard skips the next state machine sleep, so a
  Soft Reset or Hard Reset follows within tSoftReset or tHardReset, also
  when a Soft_Reset fails.
- In PE_SRC_Ready and PE_SNK_Ready a supported Message that is not
  expected there (a Status or PPS_Status not asked for, a Request to a
  Sink, Source_Capabilities to a Source, unsolicited Sink_Capabilities)
  gets a Soft Reset; it got Not_Supported, which is for unsupported
  Messages only.
- An Extended Message with Chunk Number 10..15, which is Invalid, is
  ignored; it was answered like any other. The Protocol Layer and the
  STM32 UCPD ignore it before it discards a Message of ours.
- A Soft_Reset received while the power supply transitions
  (PE_SNK_Transition_Sink, PE_SRC_Transition_Supply, the Power Role
  Swap's transition states) is a Message like any other there: Hard
  Reset Signaling, or during a Power Role Swap what the specification
  gives the port's power role. It went to PE_Soft_Reset, which during a
  swap left the port half swapped, sending Source_Capabilities with VBUS
  off. The New Source waiting for its PS_RDY to go out takes a received
  Message, whose Protocol Layer reset drops the PS_RDY without a result,
  and the end of tSrcTransOn as ErrorRecovery; it waited for ever, with
  the Type-C layer still ignoring a detach.
- A rejected DR_Swap, or one answered Not_Supported, is not sent again
  until a new Explicit Contract negotiation, a reset or a Data Role
  Swap, as for PR_Swap; a DR_Swap answered with Wait is not sent again
  within tDRSwapWait (PD_T_DR_SWAP_WAIT_MIN_MS).
- A DPM Hard Reset request in the one cycle the Protocol Layer is not
  running (after a received Soft_Reset, or the first cycle after attach)
  is kept and acted on in the next; it was dropped, so a safety Hard
  Reset the DPM asks for once could be lost.
- A Message received while PE_Chunk_Received waits out
  tChunkingNotSupported abandons the chunked Message: the Ready state
  answers the new one within tReceiverResponse. The stack has no
  Chunking layer: it follows the Protocol Layer rules of the
  specification, which let a port that needs no Message over one Chunk
  omit it with the ChunkingNotSupportedTimer, where the Extended Message
  rules disagree.
- At PD Revision 2.0 a received Extended Message or Battery_Status is
  Reserved, so it gets Reject as unsupported, not the Soft Reset an
  unexpected supported Message gets.
- A Message that discards a Source's Message sets the end-of-AMS hold
  only for an AMS the DPM started: a discarded Soft_Reset is sent again,
  and the hold it left kept SinkTxNG after the Soft Reset.
- DR_Swap gets Not_Supported unless the DPM reports a Dual-Role Data
  port (CHECK_DATA_ROLE_SWAP_SUPPORTED, a new policy check): the
  sequence is Required only of a DRD, and Reject is not a valid answer
  to a Message the port does not support. It always went to
  PE_DRS_Evaluate_Swap.
- The PPS Status Output Voltage is rounded to the nearest 20 mV; it was
  truncated.
- Timers whose maximum matters run their maximum less two state machine
  cycles (USBC_T_WITHIN_MS): tTypeCSinkWaitCap, tNoResponse,
  tChunkingNotSupported, and tPSHardReset after a Hard Reset received.
  tPSHardReset after a Hard Reset sent runs its maximum less one cycle:
  it counts from the end of the Signaling, which goes out as the timer
  starts, and its minimum, 25 ms, would let VBUS drop early. Build
  asserts keep each of these at or above its minimum for the configured
  cycle time.

Type-C layer: Attached.SRC goes to ErrorRecovery when the DPM cannot
turn VBUS on, instead of starting USB PD with VBUS off: the Type-C
Attached.SRC requirements the code quotes say the port supplies VBUS and
starts no USB PD communication before vSafe5V, and tFirstSourceCap
counts from vSafe5V.

Protocol Layer:
- The HardResetCompleteTimer runs tHardResetComplete's minimum, 4 ms,
  and its expiry wakes the port thread, and the Policy Engine runs
  without the state machine's sleep once the Hard Reset is sent or the
  timer expires: the Policy Engine Shall hear of the Hard Reset within
  tHardResetComplete. The timer was read once per 5 ms cycle and the
  Policy Engine a cycle after that, up to about 10 ms. usbc_timer_wake()
  sets a thread a timer wakes.
- A Hard Reset clears a completion left from the one before, so a Hard
  Reset requested while the Source returns to vSafe5V keeps reception
  off until the Policy Engine completes it.
- A Soft Reset no longer resets the Specification Revision, which holds
  until a Detach, Hard Reset or Error Recovery: it is set at start, not
  at every Protocol Layer reset.
- A Message with our own Port Data Role, other than GoodCRC, requests
  USB Type-C Error Recovery, except a retry of a Message already
  received (a retried Accept of our DR_Swap carries the partner's old
  role) and while the Policy Engine evaluates a DR_Swap: the GoodCRC of
  our Accept and the partner's first Message in its new role can reach
  the Protocol Layer together, before the Policy Engine switches our
  role.
- A Soft_Reset goes out at once whatever Rp says, not after the source's
  SinkTxNG and tSinkTx.
- A Message received on SOP discards any Message still waiting in the
  Protocol Layer and increments its MessageIDCounter
  (PRL_Tx_Discard_Message); only the first Message of an AMS was
  dropped, and any other was sent and then reported as discarded. A
  Sink's Message held for SinkTxOk counts as waiting until it is sent.
- Reception stays off during a Hard Reset until the Policy Engine
  reports it complete; it was turned back on at once.

STM32 UCPD: sent Hard Reset Signaling is reported as a sent message, so
the Protocol Layer does not wait out tHardResetComplete. The alert
handler ran the transmit state machine on the system work queue until
the transmitter was idle, polling the GoodCRC timer for the whole
Message and its retries, so one port held up the other's retries past
tReceive plus tRetry. It now runs the state machine while it changes
state, and the GoodCRC timer's expiry resubmits the work. A Message
received while our Message is sent or waits for its GoodCRC discards
ours, and so does a Message received while ours waits in the driver for
the line: the driver latches such a Message for the transmit state
machine apart from the event the alert handler hands to the TCPM, which
it clears first, so the discard branches now run. The driver hands the
TCPM only a Message it accepted, copied when its reception ends. The
TCPM polled the receive buffer itself every cycle, and that buffer also
holds GoodCRCs, packets with errors, SOP' packets the port does not take
and packets still arriving: a GoodCRC whose MessageID differed from the
partner's last one reached the Policy Engine as a Message, which the
handling of an unexpected Message above answers with a Soft Reset, and a
packet read half received lost its header. Only a GoodCRC on our
Message's SOP*, while that Message is out, acknowledges it, and each
attempt forgets an earlier one: each SOP* has its own MessageIDs, and a
cable's SOP' GoodCRC, or a stale one, with the same MessageID passed for
ours, so a lost Message was never retried. A retry of a Message the
driver already received, with the same MessageID on the same SOP*,
discards nothing of ours: the driver keeps the last MessageID per SOP*,
forgotten on a Soft_Reset sent or received, a Hard Reset and when
reception starts. A Protocol Layer PHY reset with reception already on
keeps a Message already acknowledged. A Ping discards none of ours. A
Power Role Swap forgets the MessageIDs received, since both sides
restart them. The end of our GoodCRC has an event of its own: sharing
the transmit result of our Message, a late work item took one for the
other and left a reply queued behind a GoodCRC already sent. A Hard
Reset we send clears every transmit result it leaves behind (a Message
it cut short reports TXMSGABT, RM0444 UCPD_SR) and stops the GoodCRC
timer, and each Message starts with no stale result: one left over
restarted the next Message in mid-transmission. A transmission the
peripheral discards on a busy line waits as for its GoodCRC: the Message
being received then discards ours once it ends, and only a busy line
with no Message spends a retry; the retries all went before the
partner's Message ended, and the Policy Engine got a transmission error.
Turning reception off, or a Hard Reset received, also ends a reception
in progress, which otherwise held every transmission until the partner
sent again. Before, our Message was retried after the partner's, and the
GoodCRC we sent for the partner's Message passed for ours. A Hard Reset
the peripheral discards on a busy line was taken for a discarded Message
and left the transmitter in its Hard Reset state, so nothing was sent
until the next Hard Reset request: it is now sent again up to three
times, as the PHY Shall once CC is idle. When the retries run out, the
driver reports a discard only if a Message was received; anything else
is a transmission error, so the Policy Engine sends a Soft Reset. It
reported a discard for any failure, the failure event being already
cleared, and the Policy Engine then waited for a Message that never
came. A received Hard Reset drops the Message in flight and its retries,
which went on during the Hard Reset.

12. support the BIST modes a USB PD port Shall support

USB PD R3.2 makes BIST reception Required of a port, and its BIST
functionality Shall be supported. The Policy Engine answered every BIST
Message with Not_Supported, an invalid BIST Test Mode included, which
the receiver Shall ignore.

From PE_SRC_Ready or PE_SNK_Ready a BIST Message now selects by its BIST
Test Mode; each mode but Shared Test Mode Exit starts only with VBUS at
vSafe5V:
- BIST Carrier Mode: PE_BIST_Carrier_Mode has the PHY transmit BIST
  Carrier Mode 2 and runs the BISTContModeTimer (tBISTContMode, 45 ms);
  at its timeout the carrier ends and the port goes to
  PE_SRC_Transition_to_default or PE_SNK_Transition_to_default. A sink
  there does not wait for the VBUS loss of a Hard Reset, which did not
  happen. The state's exit ends the carrier, so a Hard Reset, an Error
  Recovery or a detach within tBISTContMode cannot leave the PHY
  transmitting it.
- BIST Test Data: PE_BIST_Test_Mode puts the TCPC in BIST test mode,
  which answers with GoodCRC only, until a Hard Reset leaves the state.
- BIST Shared Test Mode Entry and Exit, as a source: a Shared Capacity
  Port's Master Port Shall recognize both; the Policy Engine notifies
  the DPM (BIST_SHARED_TEST_MODE_ENTRY and _EXIT), which offers, or
  stops offering, the maximum Source Capabilities on every port of the
  group. Exit is taken at any VBUS level: the Tester may have contracted
  above vSafe5V on the uncapped port, and only the entry needs vSafe5V.
  The specification conflicts on what follows the Exit: its BIST state
  machine sends the Policy Engine to PE_SRC_Transition_to_default, while
  its BIST Message rules let the UUT send new Source_Capabilities or
  perform ErrorRecovery. The port stays in PE_SRC_Ready and the DPM
  sends new Source_Capabilities, as the specification allows; the
  Tester's next contract then follows from them.
- Any other BIST Test Mode, or VBUS not at vSafe5V for one that needs
  it, is ignored.

The STM32 UCPD ends BIST Carrier Mode 2 only with the peripheral disabled
(RM0444 38.4.3): tcpc_set_bist_test_mode(false) now also ends a carrier
that runs, by disabling and enabling the UCPD, and idles its
transmitter. While the carrier runs no transmit event comes; the UCPD
alert handler, which since part 11 runs the transmit state machine only
while it changes state, returns as it does for any Message on the wire,
so the USB-C thread that ends the carrier keeps running. The disable resets every control bit (RM0444 38.8.1,
p1317), so the configuration registers, whose IFRGAP and TRANSWIN reset
to a value that is not supported, are written back while disabled, and
the control and interrupt mask registers once enabled, without the
transmit commands; on the G0 a strobe then applies the CC configuration
the control register holds (ANAMODE, RDCH, DBATTEN).

usbc_pd.h gains the BIST Data Object's Test Modes, tBISTContMode and the
vSafe5V maximum. Not run against a BIST tester.

13. let a port get its partner's Battery Status

USB PD R3.2 asks for a Get_Battery_Status in answer to an Alert with a
Battery status change (a Should), the Alert AMS's optional Get Battery
Status Sequence. The stack could answer one but never send one.

- REQUEST_PE_GET_BATTERY_STATUS, a new DPM request, goes from the Ready
  state to PE_Get_Battery_Status, at PD Revision 3.0 or later and with
  the new set_battery_status callback
  (usbc_set_policy_cb_set_battery_status()). It sends a single-chunk
  Get_Battery_Status for the Battery the last Alert with a Battery
  Status Change named (the fields are Reserved without it), the lowest
  Fixed Battery (references 0-3) or else the lowest Hot Swappable one
  (4-7), or Battery 0 before any Alert of the present partner (the
  reference is cleared when the Policy Engine starts).
- The state shares PE_Get_Status's entry, run and exit: a Battery_Status
  is reported to the DPM through the callback and the port goes back to
  Ready; a Not_Supported or Reject, a timeout, a reset or a detach
  reports NULL; any other Message is a Protocol Error.
- A Battery_Status received in a Ready state, where nothing asked for
  it, gets a Soft Reset once the port supports it.

The DPM decides when to ask: the Alert callback sees the Alert Data
Object and may request it, after the Get_Status the Alert led to or
instead of it.

14. answer Get_Source_Cap_Extended

USB PD R3.2 recommends the Get Source Capabilities Extended Responder
for a port that can operate as Source, and says the Port Shall answer a
Get_Source_Cap_Extended with a Source_Capabilities_Extended Message. The
Policy Engine answered it with Not_Supported, so a Sink could not learn
the Source's PDP rating, its touch temperature or how many Batteries it
has, which the specification points it to before a Get_Battery_Cap.

PE_GIVE_INFO now gives the Source Capabilities Extended Data Block at PD
Revision 3.0 or later, in PE_SRC_Ready and, on a Dual-Role port, in
PE_SNK_Ready (PE_SRC_Give_Source_Cap_Ext,
PE_DR_SNK_Give_Source_Cap_Ext). The block comes from a new DPM callback,
usbc_set_policy_cb_get_src_cap_ext(), with struct pd_scedb in usbc_pd.h.
Without the callback the reply stays Not_Supported. The 25-byte block
goes out as a single chunk.

15. answer Get_Manufacturer_Info

USB PD R3.2 recommends the Get Manufacturer Info Responder for every
port, and says the Port Shall answer a Get_Manufacturer_Info with a
Manufacturer_Info Message. The Policy Engine answered it with
Not_Supported.

PE_GIVE_INFO now gives the Manufacturer Info Data Block at PD Revision
3.0 or later, in either power role (PE_Give_Manufacturer_Info). The
block comes from a new DPM callback,
usbc_set_policy_cb_get_manufacturer_info(), with struct pd_midb in
usbc_pd.h; it receives the Manufacturer Info Target and Ref as sent, or
PD_BATTERY_REF_MAX for a Ref the request left out, and answers one it
does not recognize with the string "Not Supported". The Data Size covers
the string up to its NUL; the reply goes out as a single chunk. Without
the callback the reply stays Not_Supported.

16. fix what a review of the carried stack found

A review of parts 1 to 15 found these:

- A Type-C Error Recovery request, from the Device Policy Manager or the
  stack itself, is no longer queued: usbc_request() sets a flag the port
  thread runs before any queued request, so it is never refused. A Power
  Role Swap that failed while a start request was queued left the Policy
  Engine in its error submachine and the Type-C layer ignoring VBUS
  loss.
- A port thread that takes a suspend request sleeps on a semaphore until
  usbc_start() queues a start request, instead of suspending itself: a
  start that came between the port thread taking the suspend request and
  suspending was lost, and the port stayed down.
- TCPC alerts and the HardResetCompleteTimer give a semaphore that only
  the state machines' cycle sleep takes, instead of waking the port
  thread with k_wakeup(), which also cut short any sleep a Device Policy
  Manager callback took on that thread, such as the conversion wait of a
  VBUS measurement.
- Data Role Swap support follows the Dual-Role Data bit the port
  advertises, in the first PDO of the Source_Capabilities it last sent
  or of its Sink Capabilities, instead of the
  CHECK_DATA_ROLE_SWAP_SUPPORTED policy check part 11 added: a Device
  Policy Manager that did not know the check answered every DR_Swap with
  Not_Supported. The Source's bit is kept as the Capabilities are sent,
  because the get_src_caps callback may stage the caps it gives.
- PE_Send_Not_Supported notifies MSG_NOT_SUPPORTED_SENT, a new
  notification, instead of MSG_NOT_SUPPORTED_RECEIVED, which now only
  means that the partner sent Not_Supported, as its description says:
  the Device Policy Manager could not tell a partner refusing a PR_Swap,
  a DR_Swap or an Alert from a Message of the partner's that the port
  does not support.
- A Hard Reset puts the Type-C Current the Device Policy Manager chose
  on CC as it starts, instead of SinkTxOk: the Hard Reset ends the
  contract that let the Source use Rp for Collision Avoidance, and the
  port advertised 3.0 A until PE_SRC_Transition_to_default.
- A Device Policy Manager request that no state takes is logged at debug
  level, and usbc_request() says that the Policy Engine drops a request
  outside a state that can act on it; a refused request is logged at
  debug level too.
- pe_send_ext_msg() builds the single-chunk Extended Message from a data
  block, instead of four callers each writing the header and the
  padding. The PPS Status Data Block and the Source's Alert Data Object
  are sent little-endian, as the Sink's Alert Data Object already was.
- After a Wait the sink holds its Request on one deadline, tSinkRequest
  from the Wait, instead of that deadline and the SinkRequestTimer
  together; the timer now only times the DR_Swap resend after a Wait,
  and Source_Capabilities no longer cut tDRSwapWait short.
- PDO types are read through the PDO union, the APDOs a PD 2.0 partner
  does not get are logged, PD_EXT_CHUNK_NUMBER_MAX in usbc_pd.h is the
  highest valid Chunk Number for the Protocol Layer and the STM32 UCPD
  driver both, and the references of a Get_Battery_Cap,
  Get_Battery_Status or Get_Manufacturer_Info are read through the new
  union pd_get_info_db.

